WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:02.430
- Hey guys, Google had an event today

00:00:02.430 --> 00:00:04.260
where they completely surprised us

00:00:04.260 --> 00:00:06.930
by announcing the totally out of the blue

00:00:06.930 --> 00:00:11.160
Pixel 8 and Pixel 8 Pro,
and the Pixel Watch too

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:13.080
that somehow never leaked.

00:00:13.080 --> 00:00:15.780
It just completely caught us off guard.

00:00:15.780 --> 00:00:18.300
I hope you can detect my
sarcasm through the internet,

00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:19.830
but hey, we dig an event

00:00:19.830 --> 00:00:21.780
and it has some stuff that I
was really looking forward to,

00:00:21.780 --> 00:00:26.610
Pixel 8 and 8 Pro mostly
because I've been a Pixel fan

00:00:26.610 --> 00:00:30.570
even though I know it has these big flaws

00:00:30.570 --> 00:00:32.940
like the Tensor G2 had subpar performance

00:00:32.940 --> 00:00:34.590
and the somewhat average battery life.

00:00:34.590 --> 00:00:36.600
But like I still like the Pixel

00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:37.830
and there's a lot that
I was looking forward to

00:00:37.830 --> 00:00:39.000
for the next stuff.

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:41.310
So, they delivered on a lot of that stuff

00:00:41.310 --> 00:00:43.290
but it also comes with a new price.

00:00:43.290 --> 00:00:45.930
So, okay, on the outside Pixel 8 and 8 Pro

00:00:45.930 --> 00:00:48.150
do still look like Pixels, right?

00:00:48.150 --> 00:00:50.100
I told you they were sticking
with this design language

00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:52.380
with the visor because
it's very recognizable

00:00:52.380 --> 00:00:53.910
and they finally have that going for them.

00:00:53.910 --> 00:00:55.800
But this year it just feels like

00:00:55.800 --> 00:00:57.600
they rounded everything off

00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:00.450
like that's the big visible
design update this year,

00:01:00.450 --> 00:01:01.740
everything's a bit more rounded.

00:01:01.740 --> 00:01:03.150
And then there's the new colors.

00:01:03.150 --> 00:01:05.160
Now, honestly, I'm just gonna say it.

00:01:05.160 --> 00:01:06.840
I think it's been a pretty boring year

00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:09.210
for phone colors across the board,

00:01:09.210 --> 00:01:10.470
just putting it out there.

00:01:10.470 --> 00:01:12.960
But with this one it's Hazel, Rose,

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:14.640
and Obsidian for the Pixel 8

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:16.770
and then Obsidian, Porcelain,

00:01:16.770 --> 00:01:18.630
and this new blue color called Bay.

00:01:18.630 --> 00:01:20.190
I think the blue is probably
the most interesting

00:01:20.190 --> 00:01:21.023
of the whole lineup

00:01:21.023 --> 00:01:22.590
combined with the new matte finish

00:01:22.590 --> 00:01:24.090
that's on the back of all the Pros.

00:01:24.090 --> 00:01:25.050
I love that.

00:01:25.050 --> 00:01:27.750
I think it might be my
favorite alongside Matte Black.

00:01:27.750 --> 00:01:29.010
Maybe it's a toss up between the two.

00:01:29.010 --> 00:01:30.030
But then around the front

00:01:30.030 --> 00:01:31.950
both displays have actually gotten better.

00:01:31.950 --> 00:01:33.690
So the bezels are a bit thinner

00:01:33.690 --> 00:01:35.760
and they look pretty uniform
all the way around to me.

00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:38.190
And then they're both
dramatically brighter

00:01:38.190 --> 00:01:41.670
up to 2,000 nits peak
brightness on the Pixel 8

00:01:41.670 --> 00:01:44.880
and 2,400 nits on the 8 Pro.

00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:47.880
I think that's the highest
I've ever seen in a phone.

00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:49.680
So, I think it should look really good

00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:51.090
both indoors and outdoors.

00:01:51.090 --> 00:01:52.620
And then also the smaller Pixel 8

00:01:52.620 --> 00:01:54.420
is now 120 hertz as well.

00:01:54.420 --> 00:01:55.253
Love that.

00:01:55.253 --> 00:01:58.110
I know people love just
dismissing 120 hertz

00:01:58.110 --> 00:02:00.270
on cheaper phones but we're there.

00:02:00.270 --> 00:02:01.680
Now it's not LTPO yet,

00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:02.940
that's still just the pro.

00:02:02.940 --> 00:02:04.530
But yeah, just looking at these screens

00:02:04.530 --> 00:02:05.940
for the limited time
that I spent with them

00:02:05.940 --> 00:02:08.250
I'm very excited to get
them out in the real world.

00:02:08.250 --> 00:02:10.590
They're calling it an Actua Display

00:02:10.590 --> 00:02:12.450
and a Super Actua Display.

00:02:12.450 --> 00:02:14.790
Not sure why, but I guess
you gotta name everything.

00:02:14.790 --> 00:02:16.710
But anyway, this year the really big

00:02:16.710 --> 00:02:18.630
physical change we're
getting with these phones

00:02:18.630 --> 00:02:21.000
I'm gonna say is the new Tensor G3 chip.

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:23.790
So, it's the new third
generation of Google Silicon

00:02:23.790 --> 00:02:26.250
paired with eight gigs
of RAM on the Pixel 8

00:02:26.250 --> 00:02:28.830
and 12 gigs of RAM on the Pixel 8 Pro.

00:02:28.830 --> 00:02:32.340
And to be honest, Google didn't
really talk that much about,

00:02:32.340 --> 00:02:33.720
or at least flex very much

00:02:33.720 --> 00:02:36.810
of like just raw performance improvements.

00:02:36.810 --> 00:02:38.130
Like it's not really about that.

00:02:38.130 --> 00:02:39.450
I'm gonna have to test these phones

00:02:39.450 --> 00:02:41.250
to see if they actually improved a lot

00:02:41.250 --> 00:02:44.700
as far as CPU and GPU,
and just overall power.

00:02:44.700 --> 00:02:46.290
So definitely get subscribed
to be first to see

00:02:46.290 --> 00:02:48.000
those reviews when they come out.

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:51.210
Also here are the battery sizes,

00:02:51.210 --> 00:02:52.830
the new batteries of these new phones.

00:02:52.830 --> 00:02:55.980
They're both a tiny bit
bigger than last year.

00:02:55.980 --> 00:02:58.290
But really the story with the Tensor G3

00:02:58.290 --> 00:02:59.880
is there is way more TPU

00:02:59.880 --> 00:03:02.910
and way more processing
power for AI-related tasks

00:03:02.910 --> 00:03:05.370
and that checks out
because the Pixel story

00:03:05.370 --> 00:03:08.010
has always been all about the software,

00:03:08.010 --> 00:03:10.110
and that's still super true with these.

00:03:10.110 --> 00:03:11.460
There is so much software.

00:03:11.460 --> 00:03:13.170
Like the Pixel is basically

00:03:13.170 --> 00:03:15.030
the smartest smartphone in the room.

00:03:15.030 --> 00:03:18.330
It's not going to wow
you with amazing numbers

00:03:18.330 --> 00:03:20.070
or incredible build quality,

00:03:20.070 --> 00:03:22.470
or huge hardware features
or anything like that.

00:03:22.470 --> 00:03:23.400
It's gonna wow you

00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:26.310
with very clever software at every corner.

00:03:26.310 --> 00:03:27.450
So here we're talking

00:03:27.450 --> 00:03:29.280
there's a new Google Assistant feature

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:31.140
where if you're on a webpage in Chrome

00:03:31.140 --> 00:03:35.010
you can ask the assistant to
summarize the webpage for you

00:03:35.010 --> 00:03:36.270
and then using the same tech,

00:03:36.270 --> 00:03:37.800
I imagine that's in Google Bard,

00:03:37.800 --> 00:03:40.620
it'll quickly give you a three
or four bullet point summary

00:03:40.620 --> 00:03:42.840
that's hopefully accurate.

00:03:42.840 --> 00:03:45.990
A speech-to-text is also now
even faster and more accurate.

00:03:45.990 --> 00:03:47.100
It's completely on device

00:03:47.100 --> 00:03:49.740
and does a much better
job also at knowing that

00:03:49.740 --> 00:03:52.860
ums and uhs are not part
of your actual sentence,

00:03:52.860 --> 00:03:54.030
so it doesn't write them down.

00:03:54.030 --> 00:03:54.863
That's smart.

00:03:54.863 --> 00:03:56.280
They're doing more stuff
with the front facing camera

00:03:56.280 --> 00:03:57.510
to make stuff like face unlock

00:03:57.510 --> 00:03:59.610
and unlocking stuff in apps more secure.

00:03:59.610 --> 00:04:01.140
There's also a new fix-it feature

00:04:01.140 --> 00:04:03.810
which will proofread your
messages built into the keyboard

00:04:03.810 --> 00:04:06.390
before you send them with things
like grammar and all that.

00:04:06.390 --> 00:04:10.260
One of the crazy ones is
the new call screen voice.

00:04:10.260 --> 00:04:11.370
So if you've used a Pixel

00:04:11.370 --> 00:04:12.510
you know about call screen before

00:04:12.510 --> 00:04:14.190
which is you're getting a
call, it's an unknown number.

00:04:14.190 --> 00:04:16.350
You just let the Google
Assistant screen the call.

00:04:16.350 --> 00:04:20.010
The new one has a much more human voice

00:04:20.010 --> 00:04:22.980
that I honestly don't really
know if it's a robot voice

00:04:22.980 --> 00:04:24.150
or just a recording of a human

00:04:24.150 --> 00:04:26.160
but it's much more human sounding.

00:04:26.160 --> 00:04:29.580
And then also it has the chips that pop up

00:04:29.580 --> 00:04:31.830
that you can hit to respond to people

00:04:31.830 --> 00:04:33.150
that are now context aware.

00:04:33.150 --> 00:04:36.420
So if it listens to somebody
say, "Hey, I'm a UPS driver."

00:04:36.420 --> 00:04:38.227
You can now hit a button
that immediately says,

00:04:38.227 --> 00:04:40.530
"Oh, you can leave the
package at the doorstep."

00:04:40.530 --> 00:04:42.120
I got to record this.

00:04:42.120 --> 00:04:43.450
I'll just let you listen to it.

00:04:43.450 --> 00:04:44.340
- [Google Assistant]
Hi, I'm a Google Virtual

00:04:44.340 --> 00:04:45.720
Calling Assistant recording this call

00:04:45.720 --> 00:04:47.250
on behalf of the person
you're trying to reach.

00:04:47.250 --> 00:04:49.950
Can you say what you're calling about?

00:04:49.950 --> 00:04:52.912
- [Courier] I'm trying to
drop off a package for Kenny.

00:04:52.912 --> 00:04:55.245
(no audio)

00:04:57.277 --> 00:04:58.170
- [Google Assistant] They
said you can go ahead

00:04:58.170 --> 00:05:00.203
and leave the package by the front door.

00:05:00.203 --> 00:05:02.010
(no audio)

00:05:02.010 --> 00:05:02.843
- [Courier] Okay, thanks.

00:05:02.843 --> 00:05:03.918
That's great.

00:05:03.918 --> 00:05:06.251
(no audio)

00:05:08.505 --> 00:05:10.284
- [Google Assistant]
Great, I'll see you soon.

00:05:10.284 --> 00:05:11.117
(beeps)

00:05:11.117 --> 00:05:12.540
- So yeah, that's pretty good to me.

00:05:12.540 --> 00:05:14.700
It sounds very human,
but again, I don't know,

00:05:14.700 --> 00:05:17.280
it could just literally
be a human recording.

00:05:17.280 --> 00:05:19.830
Either way, it says it's
a virtual assistant.

00:05:19.830 --> 00:05:23.280
But what would a Pixel be
without some camera magic?

00:05:23.280 --> 00:05:25.620
So, let's just jump into
the new camera stuff

00:05:25.620 --> 00:05:28.980
with the Pixel 'cause there's
a lot of software magic here,

00:05:28.980 --> 00:05:29.970
lot of software magic.

00:05:29.970 --> 00:05:32.010
So the Pixel 8 and 8 Pro now share

00:05:32.010 --> 00:05:35.490
the same upgraded 50
megapixel main camera system.

00:05:35.490 --> 00:05:38.130
Then the 8 also has a
12 megapixel ultra wide

00:05:38.130 --> 00:05:40.200
but the 8 Pro just sends it all the way

00:05:40.200 --> 00:05:42.210
with a 48 megapixel ultra wide

00:05:42.210 --> 00:05:45.030
and a 48 megapixel 5x telephoto.

00:05:45.030 --> 00:05:47.160
And I also really like
the new camera app layout

00:05:47.160 --> 00:05:50.910
which is now separated basically
into photo and video mode.

00:05:50.910 --> 00:05:52.590
And then all the various settings

00:05:52.590 --> 00:05:54.240
and modes are inside of each.

00:05:54.240 --> 00:05:56.010
So I think it makes a
lot of intuitive sense.

00:05:56.010 --> 00:05:57.120
You could just switch to photo

00:05:57.120 --> 00:05:58.620
and then go to a specific photo mode.

00:05:58.620 --> 00:05:59.820
There's manual controls.

00:05:59.820 --> 00:06:01.620
It's not the first time I've
seen it laid out this way

00:06:01.620 --> 00:06:03.900
but when you get so much
stuff happening in the camera

00:06:03.900 --> 00:06:05.670
you don't wanna move it out or hide it,

00:06:05.670 --> 00:06:08.070
you just need to sort of
organize it in a capable way.

00:06:08.070 --> 00:06:08.940
I think this does a good job.

00:06:08.940 --> 00:06:12.990
So I'll have to save my photo
and video quality analysis

00:06:12.990 --> 00:06:13.950
for the full review.

00:06:13.950 --> 00:06:16.710
But like I said, there's
a lot of software features

00:06:16.710 --> 00:06:17.820
that they've added that just seem,

00:06:17.820 --> 00:06:20.520
they just get more and
more wild as you go on.

00:06:20.520 --> 00:06:23.880
So the really insane
one is the Magic Editor.

00:06:23.880 --> 00:06:24.990
So you've probably already seen,

00:06:24.990 --> 00:06:27.240
you can erase people from
the background of photos

00:06:27.240 --> 00:06:28.680
with Magic Eraser

00:06:28.680 --> 00:06:31.770
but now this Magic Editor
will let you straight up

00:06:31.770 --> 00:06:34.800
select subjects and just move them around,

00:06:34.800 --> 00:06:38.340
and completely alter the entire
photo with just a few taps.

00:06:38.340 --> 00:06:40.620
So they showed me this example
of like moving a subject

00:06:40.620 --> 00:06:42.360
to a different spot in
a photo on the beach

00:06:42.360 --> 00:06:43.800
but there's also all kinds of other demos

00:06:43.800 --> 00:06:45.300
of like changing the sky

00:06:45.300 --> 00:06:48.660
or changing shadows or
all kinds of other stuff.

00:06:48.660 --> 00:06:50.790
Honestly, it seems like
nothing is off limits.

00:06:50.790 --> 00:06:52.980
And then there's also a
feature I didn't get footage of

00:06:52.980 --> 00:06:54.990
but it's called Best Take,

00:06:54.990 --> 00:06:56.790
which is like when you're taking a photo

00:06:56.790 --> 00:06:59.040
of like a group of a bunch of people

00:06:59.040 --> 00:07:00.180
and you're like, "All right everybody,

00:07:00.180 --> 00:07:02.040
try to smile at the same time."

00:07:02.040 --> 00:07:03.720
And you take a bunch of photos

00:07:03.720 --> 00:07:05.760
but there's always somebody
blinking in one of them.

00:07:05.760 --> 00:07:08.310
It's never perfect with everyone's faces.

00:07:08.310 --> 00:07:09.450
You probably see where this is going.

00:07:09.450 --> 00:07:12.660
So Google detects that
you're taking a burst photo

00:07:12.660 --> 00:07:13.860
of a bunch of people

00:07:13.860 --> 00:07:15.540
and it will take all of those together,

00:07:15.540 --> 00:07:19.020
and give you the ability
to tap on people's faces

00:07:19.020 --> 00:07:21.300
and change them to a different instance

00:07:21.300 --> 00:07:23.700
of a different face that they're
making at a different time

00:07:23.700 --> 00:07:26.700
just to stitch together
this Frankenstein photo

00:07:26.700 --> 00:07:28.980
with everyone having perfect faces

00:07:28.980 --> 00:07:31.410
even though it's a moment
that never happened.

00:07:31.410 --> 00:07:32.580
What is a photo? (chuckles)

00:07:32.580 --> 00:07:36.000
But then there's another
one called Audio Eraser.

00:07:36.000 --> 00:07:37.200
It's a video editing feature.

00:07:37.200 --> 00:07:39.060
It's just on the Pixel 8 and 8 Pro

00:07:39.060 --> 00:07:40.380
but you can actually use it

00:07:40.380 --> 00:07:43.140
on any video you've ever
taken in your library.

00:07:43.140 --> 00:07:45.420
But that literally just
erases background sounds

00:07:45.420 --> 00:07:47.310
from like a video of a subject.

00:07:47.310 --> 00:07:48.480
It supposedly works really well,

00:07:48.480 --> 00:07:49.740
I gotta test this on more stuff

00:07:49.740 --> 00:07:51.480
but the one they showed
me worked amazingly well.

00:07:51.480 --> 00:07:53.460
And all of this is
basically along the theme of

00:07:53.460 --> 00:07:57.420
awesome software magic
enabled by Tensor G3.

00:07:57.420 --> 00:07:59.430
Like all of the neural cores

00:07:59.430 --> 00:08:02.250
and all the things that it's
doing with AI processing

00:08:02.250 --> 00:08:04.740
is what's enabling all of this awesome,

00:08:04.740 --> 00:08:07.620
weird, fancy software magic stuff.

00:08:07.620 --> 00:08:08.790
And then there's two other little things

00:08:08.790 --> 00:08:10.710
that didn't really fit
into the rest of this video

00:08:10.710 --> 00:08:14.130
but one is there's a new hole
on the back of the Pro phone

00:08:14.130 --> 00:08:15.120
as you could probably see,

00:08:15.120 --> 00:08:18.060
and that is a temperature sensor.

00:08:18.060 --> 00:08:18.900
Yeah.

00:08:18.900 --> 00:08:19.770
Actually.

00:08:19.770 --> 00:08:22.410
So, there's a new
thermometer app on the phone

00:08:22.410 --> 00:08:25.560
and when you open it, it
asks you to select a material

00:08:25.560 --> 00:08:27.420
to measure the surface temperature of

00:08:27.420 --> 00:08:28.800
I guess to be the most accurate.

00:08:28.800 --> 00:08:29.970
So you pick your material

00:08:29.970 --> 00:08:32.430
and then you just get
up close to your subject

00:08:32.430 --> 00:08:35.580
like within two inches
of it and tap to measure,

00:08:35.580 --> 00:08:38.820
and you get your temperature,
which is it works.

00:08:38.820 --> 00:08:39.930
It's pretty neat I guess.

00:08:39.930 --> 00:08:42.480
I don't know that I
would use this very much.

00:08:42.480 --> 00:08:45.420
You may also notice
that humans are missing

00:08:45.420 --> 00:08:47.340
from the object selection menu

00:08:47.340 --> 00:08:49.020
because measuring human temperature

00:08:49.020 --> 00:08:51.060
is still awaiting FDA approval.

00:08:51.060 --> 00:08:52.470
Maybe that'll get added to the menu

00:08:52.470 --> 00:08:54.030
when it gets approved later this year.

00:08:54.030 --> 00:08:54.870
Although I guess technically

00:08:54.870 --> 00:08:57.360
you could just tell it you're a ceramic

00:08:57.360 --> 00:08:58.680
or like a hot liquid or something

00:08:58.680 --> 00:08:59.730
and measure yourself anyway.

00:08:59.730 --> 00:09:00.570
I don't know.

00:09:00.570 --> 00:09:02.160
Whatever.

00:09:02.160 --> 00:09:04.500
There's a temperature
sensor now on the 8 Pro.

00:09:04.500 --> 00:09:08.130
And but the other thing is
number two, the haptics.

00:09:08.130 --> 00:09:10.020
Oh my god, I was typing on these phones

00:09:10.020 --> 00:09:12.450
and this is like the
number one thing I noticed

00:09:12.450 --> 00:09:15.870
on the 8 Pro is the haptics
are so much stronger.

00:09:15.870 --> 00:09:17.400
I mean I was just poking around the UI

00:09:17.400 --> 00:09:19.950
and scrolling a bunch, but
especially when I was typing,

00:09:19.950 --> 00:09:23.550
the haptics are super
strong and really refined.

00:09:23.550 --> 00:09:25.290
So there's a difference
between strong haptics

00:09:25.290 --> 00:09:27.000
and like clean, refined haptics.

00:09:27.000 --> 00:09:30.240
This was both and I think
that's super underrated.

00:09:30.240 --> 00:09:31.650
I don't know if they're gonna mention this

00:09:31.650 --> 00:09:33.180
or put it in the
marketing materials at all

00:09:33.180 --> 00:09:35.190
but I just love the haptics

00:09:35.190 --> 00:09:37.590
from the hour or two that
I spent with this phone.

00:09:37.590 --> 00:09:38.730
All right, quick interjection.

00:09:38.730 --> 00:09:39.840
There's actually one more thing

00:09:39.840 --> 00:09:40.920
I wanted to mention with these

00:09:40.920 --> 00:09:43.710
that's called Video Boost

00:09:43.710 --> 00:09:45.180
and it's one of the weirdest,

00:09:45.180 --> 00:09:46.080
I don't even know if I can call it

00:09:46.080 --> 00:09:47.220
a software feature actually

00:09:47.220 --> 00:09:48.480
but it's one of the weirdest things yet.

00:09:48.480 --> 00:09:51.960
So first of all, it's only
available on the Pro Pixel.

00:09:51.960 --> 00:09:54.480
Second of all, it doesn't
actually launch with the phone.

00:09:54.480 --> 00:09:56.970
It's coming soon or later.

